## 3 PSK-Core

PSK-Core is the program in A-normal form: one typed operation per line, the parameter trees explicit, a `stack{L}` a `fold`.

**A site is named `<function>/%<n>`**, `n` the number of its variable, whether the `let` has a source name (`ms`) or not (`%5`). That name is the thread every later section follows: the envelope, the sensitivities and the realization are keyed by it, a nest is named by the site it computes, and the C program comments each launch with it.

The functions `MiniGPT.apply` reaches (7 of the program's 19):

```text
fn LayerNorm.apply{D}(θ : params LayerNorm{D}, x : ℝ[..., model: D], eps : f64) -> ℝ[..., model: D] {
  μ : ℝ[...] = mean axis model x
  %4 : ℝ[..., model: D] = sub x, μ
  %5 : ℝ[..., model: D] = pow 2 %4
  σ2 : ℝ[...] = mean axis model %5
  %7 : ℝ[..., model: D] = sub x, μ
  %8 : ℝ[] = real eps
  %9 : ℝ[...] = add σ2, %8
  %10 : ℝ[...] = sqrt %9
  x̂ : ℝ[..., model: D] = div %7, %10
  γ : ℝ[D] = θ.γ
  %13 : ℝ[..., model: D] = mul x̂, γ
  β : ℝ[D] = θ.β
  %15 : ℝ[..., model: D] = add %13, β
  return %15
}

fn Linear.apply{In Out}(θ : params Linear{In, Out}, x : ℝ[..., In]) -> ℝ[..., Out] {
  w : ℝ[In, Out] = θ.w
  %3 : ℝ[..., Out] = dot x, w
  b : ℝ[Out] = θ.b
  %5 : ℝ[..., Out] = add %3, b
  return %5
}

fn MLP.apply{D hidden}(θ : params MLP{D, hidden}, x : ℝ[..., D]) -> ℝ[..., D] {
  l1 : params Linear{D, hidden} = θ.l1
  %3 : ℝ[..., hidden] = call Linear.apply{In = D, Out = hidden}(l1, x)
  %4 : ℝ[..., hidden] = gelu %3
  l2 : params Linear{hidden, D} = θ.l2
  %6 : ℝ[..., D] = call Linear.apply{In = hidden, Out = D}(l2, %4)
  return %6
}

fn SelfAttention.apply{B T}(θ : params SelfAttention, x : ℝ[batch: B, seq: T, model: 16], mask : bool[seq: T, seq2: T]) -> ℝ[batch: B, seq: T, model: 16] {
  wq : params Linear{16, 16} = θ.wq
  %4 : ℝ[batch: B, seq: T, 16] = call Linear.apply{In = 16, Out = 16}(wq, x)
  q : ℝ[batch: B, heads: 2, seq: T, hd: 8] = split_heads 2x8 %4
  wk : params Linear{16, 16} = θ.wk
  %7 : ℝ[batch: B, seq: T, 16] = call Linear.apply{In = 16, Out = 16}(wk, x)
  k : ℝ[batch: B, heads: 2, seq: T, hd: 8] = split_heads 2x8 %7
  wv : params Linear{16, 16} = θ.wv
  %10 : ℝ[batch: B, seq: T, 16] = call Linear.apply{In = 16, Out = 16}(wv, x)
  v : ℝ[batch: B, heads: 2, seq: T, hd: 8] = split_heads 2x8 %10
  %12 : ℝ[batch: B, heads: 2, seq: T, seq2: T] = ein[batch, heads, seq, seq2]([batch, heads, seq, hd] * [batch, heads, seq2, hd]) q, k
  %13 : nat = 8
  %14 : ℝ[] = real %13
  %15 : ℝ[] = sqrt %14
  s : ℝ[batch: B, heads: 2, seq: T, seq2: T] = div %12, %15
  a : ℝ[batch: B, heads: 2, seq: T, seq2: T] = softmax axis seq2 s, mask
  o : ℝ[batch: B, heads: 2, seq: T, hd: 8] = ein[batch, heads, seq, hd]([batch, heads, seq, seq2] * [batch, heads, seq2, hd]) a, v
  %19 : ℝ[batch: B, seq: T, model: 16] = merge_heads 2x8 o
  wo : params Linear{16, 16} = θ.wo
  %21 : ℝ[batch: B, seq: T, 16] = call Linear.apply{In = 16, Out = 16}(wo, %19)
  return %21
}

fn Block.apply{B T}(θ : params Block, x : ℝ[batch: B, seq: T, model: 16], mask : bool[seq: T, seq2: T]) -> ℝ[batch: B, seq: T, model: 16] {
  ln1 : params LayerNorm{16} = θ.ln1
  %4 : f64 = 1e-5
  %5 : ℝ[batch: B, seq: T, model: 16] = call LayerNorm.apply{D = 16}(ln1, x, %4)
  attn : params SelfAttention = θ.attn
  %7 : ℝ[batch: B, seq: T, model: 16] = call SelfAttention.apply{B = B, T = T}(attn, %5, mask)
  x : ℝ[batch: B, seq: T, model: 16] = add x, %7
  ln2 : params LayerNorm{16} = θ.ln2
  %10 : f64 = 1e-5
  %11 : ℝ[batch: B, seq: T, model: 16] = call LayerNorm.apply{D = 16}(ln2, x, %10)
  mlp : params MLP{16, 64} = θ.mlp
  %13 : ℝ[batch: B, seq: T, 16] = call MLP.apply{D = 16, hidden = 64}(mlp, %11)
  %14 : ℝ[batch: B, seq: T, model: 16] = add x, %13
  return %14
}

fn Embedding.apply{B T}(θ : params Embedding, ids : idx[64][batch: B, seq: T]) -> ℝ[batch: B, seq: T, model: 16] {
  tok : ℝ[64, 16] = θ.tok
  e : ℝ[batch: B, seq: T, 16] = embed tok, ids
  pos : ℝ[T, 16] = θ.pos
  %5 : idx[T][seq: T] = positions seq
  p : ℝ[seq: T, 16] = embed pos, %5
  %7 : ℝ[batch: B, seq: T, 16] = add e, p
  return %7
}

fn MiniGPT.apply{B T}(θ : params MiniGPT, ids : idx[64][batch: B, seq: T]) -> ℝ[batch: B, seq: T, vocab: 64] {
  mask : bool[seq: T, seq2: T] = causal_mask seq
  emb : params Embedding = θ.emb
  h0 : ℝ[batch: B, seq: T, model: 16] = call Embedding.apply{B = B, T = T}(emb, ids)
  blocks : params stack{2}(Block) = θ.blocks
  hL : ℝ[batch: B, seq: T, model: 16] = fold 2 Block.apply{B = B, T = T}(stacked blocks; acc h0; mask)
  lnf : params LayerNorm{16} = θ.lnf
  %8 : f64 = 1e-5
  %9 : ℝ[batch: B, seq: T, model: 16] = call LayerNorm.apply{D = 16}(lnf, hL, %8)
  head : ℝ[16, 64] = θ.head
  %11 : ℝ[batch: B, seq: T, 64] = dot %9, head
  return %11
}
```

*Command:* `presk dump ../../tests/examples/minigpt/minigpt.psk --stage core --const V=64 --const D=16 --const H=2 --const L=2`

## 4 Differentiation

Differentiation turns each `grad` into a reverse sweep: a `.vjp` that runs the function forward, keeps what the sweep needs (`--remat store`) or recomputes it (`--remat recompute`), and propagates the cotangents back.

`MiniGPT.apply` calls no `grad`, so its compiled program holds no derivative.

## 5 The e-graph optimizer

The optimizer saturates an e-graph of each function with the rewrite rules and extracts the cheapest form under its cost model.

Not run: `--opt` is off.

## 6 Precision: from a budget to a type per site

A budget bounds the distance between what the machine computes and the mathematics. Four steps turn it into types: **measure** how large the values get (the envelope), **rank** where error matters (the sensitivities), **solve** for the cheapest types the estimate allows, then **discharge** the budget by running the candidate against the exact reference.

### 6.1 The envelope

The exact interpreter runs the entry once and records the largest |x| at every site, and at each sum and contraction what its terms add up to without cancelling: divided by the sum, its condition number.

```text
presk calibrate: 69 sites over 1 samples of `MiniGPT.apply` at 128 bits
  LayerNorm.apply/%5           9.6832e0
  SelfAttention.apply/%14      8.0000e0
  SelfAttention.apply/%12      7.6050e0
  Block.apply/%8               3.5261e0
  Linear.apply/%3              3.2635e0
presk calibrate: 5 sums and contractions, worst condition numbers Σ|xᵢwᵢ| / |Σxᵢwᵢ|:
  LayerNorm.apply/%3           2.0892e0
  SelfAttention.apply/%12      1.7225e0
  Linear.apply/%3              1.3410e0
  LayerNorm.apply/%6           1.0000e0
  SelfAttention.apply/%18      1.0000e0
presk calibrate: the largest rounding error of each site's values (D155): 62 sites, 5 exact in bf16, 6 below a tenth of u·max|x| there
  site                              max|x|         f32        bf16 bf16/u|x|
  Block.apply/%3                  1.0000e0    0.0000e0    0.0000e0     0.000
  Block.apply/%9                  1.0000e0    0.0000e0    0.0000e0     0.000
  LayerNorm.apply/%12             1.0000e0    0.0000e0    0.0000e0     0.000
  MiniGPT.apply/%7                1.0000e0    0.0000e0    0.0000e0     0.000
  SelfAttention.apply/%14         8.0000e0    0.0000e0    0.0000e0     0.000
  SelfAttention.apply/%15         2.8284e0   4.8406e-8   3.0212e-4     0.027
  MiniGPT.apply/%5                1.0000e0   1.4880e-8   9.7637e-4     0.250
  Block.apply/%10                1.0000e-5  2.5262e-13   1.3580e-8     0.348
  Block.apply/%4                 1.0000e-5  2.5262e-13   1.3580e-8     0.348
  LayerNorm.apply/%8             1.0000e-5  2.5262e-13   1.3580e-8     0.348
```

The envelope holds 69 sites from **1 sample** on the initial weights, at 128 bits; every bound below is conditional on it, and says so.

*Command:* `presk calibrate --precision ../../tests/examples/minigpt/minigpt.psk --entry MiniGPT.apply --dim B=2 --dim T=8 --const V=64 --const D=16 --const H=2 --const L=2 --bits 128 --out $DOC/artifacts/envelope.json`

### 6.2 The sensitivities

`‖∂L/∂v‖₁` at each site, from one reverse sweep: how much an error at the site moves the output.

None: `MiniGPT.apply` returns no scalar `ℝ[]`, and a sensitivity is `∂L/∂v` of a scalar `L`.

### 6.3 The realization

A realization gives each site a machine type; a site it does not name takes the default.

Given: every site in `f32`.

### 6.4 The bound and the measurement

Under the realization, each rule's bound on the output's error, beside the gap measured against the exact reference.

```text
presk check --precision: `MiniGPT.apply` under f32
  magnitude   0.0000e0
  bound    k         6.5236e13  (inf relative)
  bound    normwise  8.7882e-7  (inf relative)
  bound    cond      0.0000e0  (0.00e0 relative)
  estimate rss       0.0000e0  (0.00e0 relative)
  measured             0.0000e0  (NaN relative)  the kernel interpreter against the exact reference
  envelope    $DOC/artifacts/envelope.json over 1 samples, 69 sites, initial weights
site                              magnitude            k     relative    condition
MiniGPT.apply/%11                   0.000e0     6.524e13          inf            -
LayerNorm.apply/%5                  9.683e0     4.287e12     4.427e11            -
LayerNorm.apply/%6                  1.806e0     4.287e12     2.374e12      1.000e0
LayerNorm.apply/%9                  1.806e0     4.287e12     2.374e12            -
LayerNorm.apply/%11                 2.895e0     4.077e12     1.408e12            -
LayerNorm.apply/%13                 2.895e0     4.077e12     1.408e12            -
LayerNorm.apply/%15                 2.895e0     4.077e12     1.408e12            -
MiniGPT.apply/%9                    2.895e0     4.077e12     1.408e12            -
```

The sound rule (`cond`) bounds the error by 0.000e0, conditional on the envelope's 1 sample; the measurement is 0.000e0, 0.0e0× under it.

*Command:* `presk check --precision $DOC/artifacts/envelope.json ../../tests/examples/minigpt/minigpt.psk --realize f32 --model all --sites 8 --measure`

## 7 Fusion

PSK-Realize groups the element-wise operations that can share one loop nest: an intermediate inside a group never reaches memory. `iN` is a group's input N, `sN` its step N.

In the functions `MiniGPT.apply` reaches, 3 groups, 5 `let`s fused away:

```text
fn LayerNorm.apply — 13 lets, 3 groups, 5 launches saved
  %5 = sub(i0,i1);pow 2(s0) [2 inputs, 2 steps]
  %10 = add(i0,i1);sqrt(s0) [2 inputs, 2 steps]
  %15 = sub(i0,i1);div(s0,i2);mul(s1,i3);add(s2,i4) [5 inputs, 4 steps]
```

Over one call, fusion takes 106 launches to 69.

**Where each group ends**, and why each element-wise value outside a group is alone: what reads it. A value joins its reader's group when every reader is in that group, is element-wise and of the same shape, and the group's expression stays under its size limit.

| Value | Line |  | Reaches memory because |
|-----------------------------------|------------------------------------|-----------------|------------------------|
| `LayerNorm.apply/%5` | `%5 : ℝ[..., model: D] = pow 2 %4` | ends a group of 2 | read by `σ2 : ℝ[...] = mean axis model %5`: a reduction |
| `LayerNorm.apply/%10` | `%10 : ℝ[...] = sqrt %9` | ends a group of 2 | read by `x̂ : ℝ[..., model: D] = div %7, %10`, of another shape: a group is one loop nest over one shape |
| `LayerNorm.apply/%15` | `%15 : ℝ[..., model: D] = add %13, β` | ends a group of 4 | the function's result |
| `Linear.apply/%5` | `%5 : ℝ[..., Out] = add %3, b` | alone | the function's result |
| `MLP.apply/%4` | `%4 : ℝ[..., hidden] = gelu %3` | alone | read by `%6 : ℝ[..., D] = call Linear.apply{In = hidden, Out = D}(l2, %4)`: a call |
| `SelfAttention.apply/%15` | `%15 : ℝ[] = sqrt %14` | alone | read by `s : ℝ[batch: B, heads: 2, seq: T, seq2: T] = div %12, %15`, of another shape: a group is one loop nest over one shape |
| `SelfAttention.apply/%16` | `s : ℝ[batch: B, heads: 2, seq: T, seq2: T] = div %12, %15` | alone | read by `a : ℝ[batch: B, heads: 2, seq: T, seq2: T] = softmax axis seq2 s, mask`: a reduction |
| `Block.apply/%8` | `x : ℝ[batch: B, seq: T, model: 16] = add x, %7` | alone | read by `%11 : ℝ[batch: B, seq: T, model: 16] = call LayerNorm.apply{D = 16}(ln2, x, %10)`: a call |
| `Block.apply/%14` | `%14 : ℝ[batch: B, seq: T, model: 16] = add x, %13` | alone | the function's result |
| `Embedding.apply/%7` | `%7 : ℝ[batch: B, seq: T, 16] = add e, p` | alone | the function's result |

*Command:* `presk dump ../../tests/examples/minigpt/minigpt.psk --stage realize --const V=64 --const D=16 --const H=2 --const L=2 --plan @$DOC/artifacts/plan.json`

## 8 The machine and the layout plan

Each nest has a space of layouts (vector lanes, a split over workers, for a contraction a register tile, a k-block, a loop order, packing). The plan takes the point the profile's cost model predicts fastest; "within 1 + u" counts the points the model cannot tell from the best.

The profile describes the machine as the cost model sees it, and holds the constants fitted on its calibration.

| Machine |  |
|----------------|------------------------|
| Workers | 10 (read from `cpu5`) |
| L1, private | 64 KiB |
| L2, private | 2048 KiB |
| L3 | 8192 KiB, shared by 5 workers |
| Vector registers | 32 × 16 bytes |
| Cache line | 64 bytes |

| Layout model |  |
|------------------------|-------------------|
| Starting a split launch (`spawn`) | 101.5 µs |
| Bandwidth from L2, L3, memory | 214, 354, 711 GB/s |
| Terms combined by | a p-norm, p = 1.059 |
| Its error on the calibration (u) | 0.132 |
| Rank correlation on the calibration | 0.984 |

The plan, as `presk plan` prints it: each launch's operands, the points of its space that lower, the best prediction, and the layout chosen. The entry was captured from its shapes alone, on 10 workers.

