## 13 Memory

What one call of the entry needs in memory, counted from the program at its dimensions and its realization's types, never a process's resident size. The host is recorded as the C export records it (§9); each buffer the call makes is live from its first write to its last read, and the inputs, which its caller holds, are counted apart. The calls are the network diagram's boxes, the iterations of a fold taken together: a call's share is what it made that is live at the peak, its own peak the most what it made weighs while it runs.

```text
presk memory: `MiniGPT.apply` at B = 2, T = 8 in f32: 76 operations, 69 launches
  parameters  35072 bytes: 8768 elements, 8768 in f32
  inputs      35200 bytes, the parameters among them
  peak        9728 bytes live, at operation 35 of 76, `MLP.apply/%4` in blocks.mlp, iteration 0
  outputs     4096 bytes
  needs       44928 bytes: the inputs and the peak
  C arena     57344 bytes, 22144 beside the inputs, 2.28 times the peak: the frames of `presk export --target c`, each function's buffers held until it returns (103360 released nothing)
  placed      9728 bytes beside the inputs: every buffer at an offset by its life, rounded to 64 (`--straight-line --placed`)
presk memory: 13 calls, their shares of the peak and their own peaks
  call                           share      own peak
  `MiniGPT.apply` itself           512
  emb                                0          2560
  blocks ×2                        9216          9216
    blocks.ln1 ×2                     0          1152
    blocks.attn ×2                    0          4096
    blocks.ln2 ×2                     0          1152
    blocks.mlp ×2                  8192          8192
  lnf                                0          1152
```

The C program's arena holds each function's buffers until it returns (D80), so it is 2.28 times the peak beside the inputs. A straight-line host with every buffer at an offset by its life takes 9728 bytes beside them; that it computes the native backend's bits, which `make c-conformance` checks, is what shows the lifetimes sound.

The same as polynomials in the entry's dimensions (plan 0.15.0 step 3), the `const`s as their numbers. Every axis of a buffer is a linear form in the dimensions, so a buffer's bytes are a polynomial; each is interpolated from recordings of the call on a grid of three values per dimension, the operations the same at every point, and checked at a point off the grid. The peak is the largest of the operations' live bytes that no other's dominates for dimensions of at least 1.

```text
presk memory --symbolic: `MiniGPT.apply` in f32, in B and T: each buffer's bytes interpolated on B ∈ {2, 4, 6}, T ∈ {8, 16, 24}, checked at B = 8, T = 32
  inputs      8 · B · T + 64 · T + 34560
  outputs     256 · B · T
  peak        the largest of 3, none of which dominates another:
                8 · B · T² + 256 · B · T + 8 · T², at operation 19 (`SelfAttention.apply/%12`)
                16 · B · T² + 128 · B · T + 8 · T² + 4, at operation 22 (`SelfAttention.apply/%16 (s)`)
                576 · B · T + 8 · T², at operation 35 (`MLP.apply/%4`)
  at B = 2, T = 8: the peak 9728, the inputs 35200, as counted
```

